.TH TUNPROXY 8 "September 2026" "TunProxy @TUNPROXY_VERSION_STRING@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
tunproxy \- manage a transparent TUN proxy through a pinned sing-box core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tunproxy
.RI [ --direct ]
.RI { on | off | status | bypass | setting }
.RI [ socks5://host:port ]
.br
.B tunproxy --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tunproxy
creates a system TUN interface and forwards TCP traffic to a configured SOCKS5
upstream. The command-line client talks to a root-owned local daemon, so
members of the sudo group do not need sudo for normal commands. It does not use
shell proxy environment variables and does not manage proxy nodes or
subscriptions.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B on
Verify or repair the managed core, probe the upstream, and enable the TUN.
Prints the same result block as
.BR status .
.TP
.B off
Stop the managed core and remove its runtime state.
.TP
.B status
Show the current state, upstream, managed core, PID, routing mode, and the
number of bypassed CIDRs.
.TP
.B bypass
List the effective bypass CIDRs. While the proxy is on, the list is the policy
pinned at startup; otherwise it is a preview computed from the current network.
.TP
.B setting
Show or update the SOCKS5 upstream. Without an argument the command prompts
for host and port.
.TP
.B --version
Show the installed TunProxy version and managed sing-box version.
.TP
.B --direct
Bypass the daemon for recovery on systems where the control service is
unavailable. This mode requires root privileges.
.SH OUTPUT
Standard output carries only the result block, one
.I Key: value
pair per line. Progress and warnings go to standard error, so scripts can
parse standard output on its own. When the core is already installed and
verified,
.B on
prints no log lines at all.
.PP
Example result block:
.PP
.nf
Status: ON
Upstream: socks5://192.168.1.20:7890 (192.168.1.20)
Core: sing-box 1.13.18
PID: 1234
Routing: auto-redirect
Bypass: 13 CIDRs
.fi
.PP
.I Routing
is
.B auto-redirect
when the kernel accepted the sing-box auto_redirect mode and
.B tun-route
when TunProxy fell back to plain TUN routing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tunproxy/config
User configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tunproxy/cores/1.13.18/sing-box
Verified private sing-box executable.
.TP
.I /run/tunproxy/
Runtime configuration, state, lock, and log files.
.TP
.I /run/tunproxy/control.sock
Local control socket. Access is limited to root and members of the sudo group.
.TP
.I /var/cache/tunproxy/
Temporary sing-box archives. The download progress bar is printed to standard
error while an archive is being fetched.
.SH INSTALLATION
Install the local Debian package with
.BR "sudo apt install ./tunproxy_VERSION_amd64.deb" .
Using apt is recommended so package dependencies are resolved. Installation
enables only
.BR tunproxy.socket .
It does not enable the proxy, create a TUN interface, start sing-box, or modify
routes.
.SH UPDATE
Install a newer Debian package directly over the installed version with
.BR "sudo apt install ./tunproxy_VERSION_amd64.deb" .
Do not remove or purge the old package first. The update safely stops an active
proxy and preserves
.IR /etc/tunproxy/config ,
verified cores under
.IR /var/lib/tunproxy/cores/ ,
and the download cache. The proxy remains off after the update until the user
explicitly runs
.BR "tunproxy on" .
Use
.B apt install --reinstall
to repair an installation with the same package version.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The current release proxies TCP traffic. DNS is sent through DNS-over-HTTPS via
the SOCKS5 upstream. UDP traffic to local and bypassed networks is direct; other
non-DNS UDP traffic is rejected.
.SH BYPASS POLICY
The bypass policy is fixed and cannot be disabled or changed through user
configuration. It always excludes loopback, private, carrier-grade NAT,
link-local, unique-local, multicast, and broadcast ranges. Before enabling the
TUN, TunProxy adds host routes only for addresses assigned to active
interfaces. The IP address selected for the SOCKS5 upstream is always added as
a direct host route. Arbitrary kernel routes are not imported.
.PP
Traffic is matched in this order:
.IP 1. 4
Selected SOCKS5 upstream address: direct.
.IP 2. 4
Fixed ranges and active interface host addresses: direct.
.IP 3. 4
Other DNS traffic: hijacked and resolved through DNS-over-HTTPS via the upstream.
.IP 4. 4
Other UDP traffic: rejected.
.IP 5. 4
Everything else: SOCKS5 upstream.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR ip (8),
.BR curl (1)
